<!--Engine Documentation — MixedStrategies
  FQN: org.blockml.bml.engine.blockrender.documentation.MixedStrategies-->
<edoc:MixedStrategies xmlns="http://blockml.org/bml"
  xmlns:core="org.blockml.bml.core"
  xmlns:edoc="org.blockml.bml.engine.blockrender.documentation">

  <baseType>
    core:Documentation
  </baseType>
  <is>
    Several strategies inside one RenderTarget
  </is>
  <documentation>
    One RenderTarget holds one engine instance (or none, if it only delegates)
    and many processors. Each processor calls the method it needs. The target is
    not labelled atomic or composite; processors plus delegates define it:

    - only own processors — the target handles matching blocks itself
    - only delegates — no engine, no processors
    - both — some blocks handled here, others forwarded

    Illustrative mix on the MachineBlocks SCAD target (the processors are owned
    by @machineblocks/render-target; V1 of that target ships the first two):

        machineblocks-v3-scad
          ScadBlockProcessor                 compile   com.machineblocks.bml.core.Block
          ScadSetProcessor                   compile   com.machineblocks.bml.core.Set
          MathBuiltInProcessor               copy      org.blockml.bml.service.Math
          CapabilityImplementationProcessor  compile   capability implementations

    Dispatch, applied by the target using the engine only after a processor matches:

    1. Own processors: exact FQN match.
    2. Own processors: baseType-chain fallback. The most specific match wins.
    3. Delegates: the same two steps, recursively.
    4. No match: diagnostic BML_NO_TARGET.

    More specific processors win over generic ones.

    A delegate-only target (example: a domain library that adds Block types and
    no new emit rules) sets engine unset, processors empty, and delegates to
    the target that already knows how to render. It does not depend on this package.

  </documentation>
  <references>
    <see type="edoc:RenderContext" relation="ReadNext" />
  </references>
</edoc:MixedStrategies>
